226
00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:43.319
Christine Sanni, Sybal: But today I will provide an overview of what makes policy language enforceable. Language, rules, sentences within policy enforceable.

227
00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:49.730
Christine Sanni, Sybal: How to write enforceable language, the sort of the components, the feature, the characteristics of

228
00:22:49.750 --> 00:23:01.380
Christine Sanni, Sybal: an enforceable rule, and then how enforceability actually improves auditability. So, with all the things that I will cover today, I'll go through some examples.

229
00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:16.359
Christine Sanni, Sybal: These are really the key things that I'd like for you to look for, in this presentation. I think they will be the most valuable to you, especially, as this committee is focused on governance and standards and policies.

230
00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:23.659
Christine Sanni, Sybal: This… these topics here will be, incredibly insightful for you.

231
00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:42.919
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So, I will start with what is an enforceable policy. So, some of you may be familiar with the term enforceability, but I will be very, very specific in that we define an enforceable policy as consisting of language, again, rules.

232
00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:46.900
Christine Sanni, Sybal: That are observable and measurable.

233
00:23:47.460 --> 00:24:02.130
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Now, what I also want to say is that it does not indicate, the language… language's effectiveness, or how well the policy itself is implemented.

234
00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:16.860
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Enforceability is specific to, can I observe the execution of this policy, and based on that observation, do I have enough information to measure how effective it is?

235
00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:29.599
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So, with that said, I want to dive into observability. What makes a rule of policy, observable?

236
00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:44.369
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Let's start with looking at an enforceable rule. Again, enforceability is observability and measurability. So, in this particular example, I have an enforceable rule. That rule is the sales representative

237
00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:49.140
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Must provide a copy of the return policy with the receipt.

238
00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:53.470
Christine Sanni, Sybal: To the customer at the time of sale.

239
00:24:55.160 --> 00:25:05.300
Christine Sanni, Sybal: When I look at this sentence, I see 3 features popping out. I've color coordinated them to make it easy for us to all see it together.

240
00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:15.699
Christine Sanni, Sybal: But when you look at what does it mean to be observable, you're looking for these… these three characteristics. The first characteristic is, is there a responsible party?

241
00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:28.360
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So, who is responsible for making the outcome of this rule or sentence or policy happen? In this example, it is the sales representative.

242
00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:37.639
Christine Sanni, Sybal: The second feature you see is what we call outcomes. What must happen as a result

243
00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:41.079
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Of the execution of this particular sentence.

244
00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:43.100
Christine Sanni, Sybal: broader policy.

245
00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:46.919
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And so, we see in this example that

246
00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:59.750
Christine Sanni, Sybal: the outcome is that a copy of the return policy has been provided with the receipt to the customer. That is the outcome. That's how we know this rule was executed.

247
00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:01.420
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And then…

248
00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:15.520
Christine Sanni, Sybal: the how. We call this the condition. It could be when this outcome must happen, where it must happen, how it must happen. And so, in this example, you have, at the time.

249
00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:16.920
Christine Sanni, Sybal: of sale.

250
00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:24.309
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And what we find about, observability is when you have…

251
00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:36.660
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Now, and have written your rule, your policy, With observable language, observable features, Two things actually happen.

252
00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:39.770
Christine Sanni, Sybal: The first thing that happens is that…

253
00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:48.390
Christine Sanni, Sybal: When you look at those features, you actually begin to understand what information You need to collect.

254
00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:55.610
Christine Sanni, Sybal: in order to observe that language. One of the…

255
00:26:56.970 --> 00:27:03.329
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Key areas, when working with clients, around…

256
00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:15.339
Christine Sanni, Sybal: writing policy, policy that is aligned with higher governance, that is regulation, guidance, statutes. It's what information do I need to collect?

257
00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:22.810
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And where do I get that information from? And so, what is so,

258
00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:36.020
Christine Sanni, Sybal: exciting about writing policy, rules, writing your language with this observability is that it immediately tells you what…

259
00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:41.699
Christine Sanni, Sybal: data, what information is needed? So, in this example.

260
00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:56.640
Christine Sanni, Sybal: your enforceable rule, the sales representative must provide a copy of the return policy with the receipt to the customer at the time of sale. When we look at the responsible party, it's clear for us that, well, we need

261
00:27:56.680 --> 00:28:03.290
Christine Sanni, Sybal: who the person is, the sales rep is, who that responsible party is. That piece of information would become

262
00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:08.369
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Really critical to observing the execution of this sentence.

263
00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:15.170
Christine Sanni, Sybal: On the outcome side, providing a copy of the return policy with the receipt.

264
00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:18.660
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Of course, you would need information like the receipt ID,

265
00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:29.910
Christine Sanni, Sybal: maybe the notice ID to see if those two things were executed and delivered in the manner they were supposed to be delivered.

266
00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:34.540
Christine Sanni, Sybal: At the… which is the last, component here, the condition.

267
00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:41.360
Christine Sanni, Sybal: time of sale, so you would need things like time of sale, sale ID, date of sale.

268
00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:46.819
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And so that observability immediately helps you understand

269
00:28:46.880 --> 00:29:05.670
Christine Sanni, Sybal: what information do I need to actually observe this sentence? But there is an additional benefit. That additional benefit is now that I'm looking at the right information, and I can observe that information, ultimately what I'm observing is the performance

270
00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:07.590
Christine Sanni, Sybal: of that rule.

271
00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:29.710
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And when you can observe the performance, like, did we do something, or did we not do something, you can actually measure. So this is the other component of enforceability. Again, remember, enforceability is not how well your policy is implemented, or even how effective it is. I'll say it in another way, in that

272
00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:35.739
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Enforceability, Does not equate to effectiveness.

273
00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:41.859
Christine Sanni, Sybal: It is not a guarantee. And so, this observation is saying that

274
00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:57.569
Christine Sanni, Sybal: you need to continue to observe so that you can measure how well that policy, that rule, that sentence is performing in operation. So that's a… it's really exciting to see now

275
00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:07.709
Christine Sanni, Sybal: fundamentally, that enforceability Gives you, now, new capabilities.

276
00:30:07.870 --> 00:30:12.880
Christine Sanni, Sybal: To be able to have a proactive governance, framework.

277
00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:31.309
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So, I want to take us into an example, and I'd like for you to meet a process owner. A process owner, responsible for authoring policy that governs the submission of spending reports. Let's say that's her role. She owns that process.

278
00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:34.460
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So this is my first question over to the audience.

279
00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:45.249
Christine Sanni, Sybal: This is her rule within this policy. The budget analyst will submit the spending report To their department supervisor.

280
00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:46.910
Christine Sanni, Sybal: as required.

281
00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:50.139
Christine Sanni, Sybal: I'm gonna turn it over to the audience really quick.

282
00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:53.370
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Is this rule actually enforceable?

283
00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:58.750
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): I think the condition is vague, as required.

284
00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:00.450
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Who is that?

285
00:31:01.250 --> 00:31:03.220
Christine Sanni, Sybal: I can't see who's talking.

286
00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:06.470
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Alright, that's Eric, and then Neil has that same comment in the chat.

287
00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:21.809
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Excellent, excellent, excellent. So you guys are really good at this, right? So I was gonna ask, what's missing here, right? So, we do have a responsible party. We know that it is the budget analyst who's responsible for executing this task.

288
00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:29.690
Christine Sanni, Sybal: We have the outcome. The spending report will be submitted, to the department supervisor.

289
00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:35.670
Christine Sanni, Sybal: But, as you stated, the condition is vague. It's ambiguous, at best.

290
00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:39.509
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And ambiguity in policy.

291
00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:46.519
Christine Sanni, Sybal: creates room for misinterpretation, right? And it does put enforceability at risk.

292
00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:54.530
Christine Sanni, Sybal: When one or more of that criteria is missing, the rule then loses enforceability.

293
00:31:54.630 --> 00:32:02.849
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Sure, you may have, okay, who's supposed to execute it? And you may have the outcome, but if the condition is missing and vague.

294
00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:16.819
Christine Sanni, Sybal: I may read this sentence and say, oh, well, I have time on Friday, I'll submit it every Friday. Or someone else may read it and say, well, as required, I'll get to it when I get to it.

295
00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:25.280
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And then, of course, in a compliance world, in an audited world, that lack of standardization

296
00:32:25.700 --> 00:32:32.439
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Then leads to failed compliance events, audit, opinion.

297
00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:42.809
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Right? And so you start to see how the language itself, the enforceability of the language, is the foundation needed for.

298
00:32:44.540 --> 00:33:02.049
Christine Sanni, Sybal: being able to have a stronger auditability posture. So, examples of ambiguous language, you pointed out, as required, immediate, like, too vague, if applicable, sufficient when appropriate, as needed.

299
00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:10.869
Christine Sanni, Sybal: when required, all the different flavors. These words create subjectivity.

300
00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:29.020
Christine Sanni, Sybal: misinterpretation, lack of standardization. And so, let's look at how to write an enforceable policy. First, it's using very definitive language, right? Use definite language to remove that subjectivity and mitigate misinterpretation.

301
00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:34.940
Christine Sanni, Sybal: I use the word mitigate because there is always the human element.

302
00:33:35.180 --> 00:33:40.039
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Right? So even if you have an enforceable policy, there is still…

303
00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:45.150
Christine Sanni, Sybal: The need to have that observation, and to

304
00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:49.079
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Based on that observation, truly measure

305
00:33:49.400 --> 00:34:00.449
Christine Sanni, Sybal: The overall effectiveness of that sentence, because there are all of the uncontrollables that create an environment where you go from being reactive

306
00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:08.510
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Learning that the policy or the language doesn't work, at the time of audit to being proactive.

307
00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:22.450
Christine Sanni, Sybal: when those uncontrollable variables happen, and still you have this policy. So, using words like must and shall and required to…

308
00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:29.899
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Those… not as required, but re… you are required to do this at this time.

309
00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:46.370
Christine Sanni, Sybal: are really, really important, right? So, the question becomes, if you have a requirement, when is the action required? You want to write that in your sentence. That is the condition. When is it applicable?

310
00:34:46.370 --> 00:35:02.550
Christine Sanni, Sybal: what is actually sufficient? So, taking it just one step further to define that. What is appropriate, right? And when is it needed? Defining these criteria, what it does is it sets the parameter of performance.

311
00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:10.369
Christine Sanni, Sybal: I don't want to move too quickly from that, because when you set the parameter of performance, you are setting the expectation.

312
00:35:11.020 --> 00:35:15.519
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And then what happens in the real world, the… what we call expression.

313
00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:19.919
Christine Sanni, Sybal: of that performance. You can now compare back to.

314
00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:27.150
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So, examples of this would be, like, must submit before 5 PM.

315
00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:28.680
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Versus…

316
00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:40.719
Christine Sanni, Sybal: submit as needed, or as required, right? A consent form is required when the patient is less than 18 years

317
00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:48.220
Christine Sanni, Sybal: of age, right? Or, instead of… when needed, Every Monday.

318
00:35:49.030 --> 00:36:00.430
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So these small shifts in language, using that definitive language, is really, really, really important, and it changes. It just immediately changes

319
00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:03.720
Christine Sanni, Sybal: The structure and the enforceability

320
00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:18.110
Christine Sanni, Sybal: of, your policy. So then when you look at policy, you're not just looking at a document, you're looking at, sentence by sentence, do I have these features of enforceability within each sentence?

321
00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:21.960
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And then… If I do.

322
00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:29.419
Christine Sanni, Sybal: I understand now what information I need to collect in order to observe and then therefore measure.

323
00:36:29.600 --> 00:36:33.380
Christine Sanni, Sybal: So this is really the making of an enforceable policy.

324
00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:46.779
Christine Sanni, Sybal: We have a responsible party, we have outcomes, we have conditions, and we also have data. So if we were to go back to our process owner's example.

325
00:36:47.050 --> 00:36:53.140
Christine Sanni, Sybal: We could improve that by taking that ambiguous language and giving it more…

326
00:36:53.430 --> 00:37:06.729
Christine Sanni, Sybal: definition, more criteria. So it could look something like this, right? The budget analyst will submit the spending report to their department supervisor every Friday.

327
00:37:07.190 --> 00:37:09.340
Christine Sanni, Sybal: it removes…

328
00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:26.400
Christine Sanni, Sybal: room for misinterpretation, it sets a clear parameter. And then, if, say, the spinning report is submitted on the following Tuesday, it's clear to see that this rule was not executed as expected.

329
00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:31.239
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And because we have that enforceability structure in the sentence.

330
00:37:31.590 --> 00:37:50.029
Christine Sanni, Sybal: we now understand what information we should collect to maintain that visibility and measurability. So I want to talk about that just for, a moment. What value does that create when you have and are collecting the right data?

331
00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:56.500
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Our work thus far here at CIBL, we've been able to directly connect

332
00:37:56.610 --> 00:38:06.110
Christine Sanni, Sybal: this, introduction of enforceability, or the presence, and the lack thereof of enforceability to audit

333
00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:09.400
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Opinion, audit results.

334
00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:21.579
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And so, I think as, an initial benefit, right, to having this enforceability be the structure of your policies and standards and controls.

335
00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:30.280
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Immediately when you're collecting that right data, you have accessibility, and you have availability.

336
00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:45.170
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And that means when you collect the right data, that data is easily accessible to the stakeholder that needs it, and readily available when needed for your audit. This is your evidentiary matter, right? So.

337
00:38:45.380 --> 00:38:48.880
Christine Sanni, Sybal: When an auditor or an examiner comes in.

338
00:38:49.200 --> 00:39:03.980
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And they are reviewing a sample size of data. You have the right data that you need, the evidentiary matter that you need, specific, not just to the policy document, but at the sentence level.

339
00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:24.199
Christine Sanni, Sybal: Which is really, really exciting. That reduces the time, it reduces the cost and the labor, that clients will expend to ready themselves for audit. The other value add is that when you are observing and collecting the right data, you can be proactive, right? Rather than reactive.

340
00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:31.530
Christine Sanni, Sybal: And find out at that compliance event that the policy as written

341
00:39:31.680 --> 00:39:49.869
Christine Sanni, Sybal: is not enforceable, not clear, not concise, right, and therefore impacting performance. So when you collect the right data, you can act before audit events, and really shift your governance, posture from reactive to proactive.

342
